"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"][8]={"school_computer_science_en:M8-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M8-L1 – Sensors, control and simulation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Systems and software projects<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (basic level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4 – From a measured signal to a\ntable<\/a>, <a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2 – States and finite\nautomata<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model sensor-actuator feedback\nand explain the limits of discrete simulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When should a heater switch?<\/h2>\n<p>A heater in a model room should switch according to measured\ntemperatures. A rule with just one threshold might switch it on and off\nfrequently when readings are close to that threshold. A\n<strong>thermostat with hysteresis<\/strong> uses two thresholds. The gap\nbetween them lets the heater retain its state for a while as readings\nfluctuate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measuring, deciding and acting<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>sensor<\/strong> measures the temperature and reports a\nvalue. The <strong>controller<\/strong> knows the thresholds and the\nprevious heater state. It determines the next state. The\n<strong>actuator<\/strong> carries out that decision by switching the\nheater on or off. This can change the room temperature. The next\nmeasurement captures the new conditions. The return path from the result\nto the next decision is called <strong>feedback<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000455_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Room temperature determines the sensor reading. The reading reaches the controller. The controller switches the heater, which affects the room.\" />\n<figcaption>The sensor feeds the room temperature into the next\ndecision.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>In this model, the heater switches on at or below 19 °C and off at or\nabove 22 °C. Between those thresholds it retains its previous state. At\n20 °C it can therefore be on while the room warms up or off while the\nroom cools down. A reading within the band alone cannot predict the\nstate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Testing with readings and with feedback<\/h2>\n<p>Given a fixed series of readings, the heater starts off. At 20 °C it\nstays off. At 19 °C it turns on, then stays on at 20 °C and 21 °C. At 22\n°C it switches off, stays off at 21 °C and switches back on at 19\n°C.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>discrete simulation<\/strong> generates its own subsequent\ntemperatures in equal time steps. We assume that the temperature rises\nby 2 °C per step while the heater is on and otherwise falls by 1 °C. The\nnext reading occurs only after this change. The program checks the fixed\nreadings and then simulates eight steps with feedback.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> next_state(reading, heating):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> reading <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">19<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> reading <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">22<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> heating<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>readings <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">19<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">21<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">22<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">21<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">19<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>heating <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>states <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> reading <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> readings:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    heating <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> next_state(reading, heating)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    states.append(heating)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> states <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>temperature <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">19<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>heating <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>trace <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    heating <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> next_state(temperature, heating)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    trace.append((temperature, heating))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    temperature <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> heating <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> trace <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"dv\">19<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">21<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">23<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">False<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">22<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">False<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"dv\">21<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">False<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">False<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">19<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">21<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>]<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>In the simulation, while the heater is on, one step takes the\ntemperature from 21 °C to 23 °C. That reading switches the heater off at\nthe next sampling instant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Start with the heater on. A sensor reports 20 °C, 22 °C, 21 °C and\n19 °C in sequence. Record the state after each reading.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Change the model so that the temperature rises by 1 °C per step\nwhile the heater is on. Starting at 19 °C, at what temperature and in\nwhich step does the heater first switch off? Count from step 0.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">On, off, off, on. The readings 20 °C and 21 °C are between the\nthresholds, so each preserves the previous state.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The readings at steps 0 through 3 are 19 °C, 20 °C, 21 °C and 22 °C.\nThe heater first switches off at 22 °C, in step 3.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions, errors and limits<\/h2>\n<p>The two threshold values belong to their respective switching cases.\nIf the code mistakenly used <code>&lt;<\/code> and <code>&gt;<\/code>, the\nresults at 19 °C and 22 °C would change. Real rooms also warm and cool\nby varying amounts. Measurement errors and delays can affect switching.\nA good experiment keeps the step size fixed and checks whether changing\nassumptions changes the result.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor, a control algorithm, an actuator and the environment form a\nfeedback loop. Hysteresis explains why the previous state matters\nbetween the two thresholds. Simulation helps us trace these transitions\nand test their assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L2 – Planning, documenting and\nversioning software<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/FA/IBS/user_upload/GI-Bildungsstandards_Informatik_Sek_I__2025__-_blau.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §5.4 · Model sensor-actuator\nfeedback and explain the limits of discrete simulation. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/Rahmenlehrplanprojekt/amtliche_Fassung/Teil_C_Informatik_2015_11_10.pdf\">Bildungsserver\nBerlin-Brandenburg · Senatsverwaltung Berlin und MBJS Brandenburg –\nRahmenlehrplan Teil C – Informatik, Wahlpflichtfach, Jahrgangsstufen\n7–10<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.9 · Model sensor-actuator feedback and\nexplain the limits of discrete simulation. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M8-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M8-L2 – Planning, documenting and versioning software<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Systems and software projects<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (basic level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5 – Test cases and debugging<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1 – Classes, objects and\ninterfaces<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Connect testable requirements,\ninterfaces, tests and version history.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What counts as done?<\/h2>\n<p>A group is building a school lending display. “The display should\nwork well” leaves the team without specific behaviour to check. “A known\nitem with no active loan is shown as available” identifies a testable\ncase. Two people can then work independently on implementation and\ntesting.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a requirement to a recorded version<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>requirement<\/strong> describes the expected benefit or\nbehaviour. An <strong>acceptance criterion<\/strong> expresses it as\nobservable conditions. An <strong>interface<\/strong> specifies the\ninputs a software component expects and the output it provides. Other\ncomponents can then use the function without knowing its internal\nrepresentation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000450_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Left to right: lending requirement, observable acceptance criterion, test case and a commit with the change and tests.\" />\n<figcaption>A testable promise connects a requirement with a test and\nits corresponding commit.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>A <strong>version control system<\/strong> such as Git records a\nselected set of file versions as a <strong>commit<\/strong>. Git\ndistinguishes three states. The working directory contains the current\nfiles. The index or staging area holds the file versions selected for\nthe next commit. The commit history contains the recorded versions of\nthe project. A small, traceable commit connects a specific change with\nthe corresponding tests.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Making a lending rule testable<\/h2>\n<p>We agree on the function\n<code>is_available(item_id, known_items, active_loans)<\/code>. It\nreturns true when the identifier is known and has no active loan. In the\nexample, <code>CAM1<\/code> and <code>CAM2<\/code> are known.\n<code>CAM2<\/code> is on loan. <code>CAM9<\/code> does not exist. When the\nresult is false, the display can give a more specific reason if it also\nknows whether the identifier exists.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> is_available(item_id, known_items, active_loans):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> item_id <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> known_items <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> item_id <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> active_loans<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>items <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&quot;CAM1&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;CAM2&quot;<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>loans <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&quot;CAM2&quot;<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> is_available(<span class=\"st\">&quot;CAM1&quot;<\/span>, items, loans) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> is_available(<span class=\"st\">&quot;CAM2&quot;<\/span>, items, loans) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> is_available(<span class=\"st\">&quot;CAM9&quot;<\/span>, items, loans) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The team documents the inputs, the result and the assumption that\nidentifiers are unique strings. In a new local Git practice directory it\nsaves the function as <code>available.py<\/code> and the checks as\n<code>tests.py<\/code>. The first line in <code>tests.py<\/code> is\n<code>from available import is_available<\/code>. This sequence assumes\nthat a local author identity is configured in Git.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>git init\npython -B tests.py\ngit add available.py tests.py\ngit diff --staged\ngit commit -m &quot;Define and test item availability&quot;\ngit status --short<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The successful test run ends without an assertion error.\n<code>git add<\/code> selects the two file versions.\n<code>git diff --staged<\/code> displays the staged changes before the\ncommit. If these are the only files and they have remained unchanged,\n<code>git status --short<\/code> produces no output afterwards.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the requirement “An item on loan may not be lent out twice,”\nwrite an acceptance criterion giving the initial situation, action and\nobservable result. Also check whether the data should remain\nunchanged.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">You stage <code>available.py<\/code> with <code>git add<\/code> and\nthen change it again. Which version will the next ordinary\n<code>git commit<\/code> contain? How can you include the new change in\nthe commit?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Initially, <code>CAM2<\/code> is assigned to the fictional identifier\n<code>A17<\/code>. A second loan is requested for <code>A18<\/code>. The\nprogram reports the rejection and still shows exactly the original\nassignment to <code>A17<\/code>. The second identifier appears in no\nactive loan for <code>CAM2<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The commit contains the file version selected by the last\n<code>git add available.py<\/code>. Running\n<code>git add available.py<\/code> again stages the new version.\n<code>git diff --staged<\/code> shows what is then staged.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions, errors and limits<\/h2>\n<p>The three tests establish only the agreed examples for this function.\nThe function reads two collections. It does not reserve an item or\nprevent simultaneous loans. A local commit records a version in the\nlocal repository. A separate backup or an approved shared location\nserves a different purpose. Test data and version history should contain\nno real personal data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>A testable criterion connects a requirement with a concrete test. The\ninterface makes the behaviour available to other components. The commit\nrecords a checked change as a traceable version.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L3 – Concurrency and consistent\nupdates<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.1.4 · Connect testable\nrequirements, interfaces, tests and version history. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Basics-Recording-Changes-to-the-Repository\">Scott\nChacon und Ben Straub – Pro Git – Recording Changes to the\nRepository<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: Recording Changes · Connect testable\nrequirements, interfaces, tests and version history. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M8-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M8-L3 – Concurrency and consistent updates<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Systems and software projects<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (advanced level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L2 – Planning, documenting and\nversioning software<\/a>, <a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L4 –\nNormalisation and data integrity<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain a lost update and\nevaluate synchronisation as a model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When two people count at once<\/h2>\n<p>Two stations record how many headphones have been returned to a\nstoreroom. Both increment the same counter. One station may already have\nread its value while the other prepares its own update. With ten devices\nin stock, two returns should bring the total to twelve. Whether that\nresult is reached depends on how the work steps interleave.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concurrent steps and shared data<\/h2>\n<p>Two activities are <strong>concurrent<\/strong> if their processing\nperiods overlap. Their individual steps may take turns. They are\n<strong>parallel<\/strong> if multiple steps actually run at the same\ntime. Alternating execution on a single processor core is enough for the\nproblem below.<\/p>\n<p>In this model, each increment consists of reading, adding and\nwriting. Reading and adding can be grouped into a local preparation step\nin this example. Writing replaces the shared value. A <strong>lost\nupdate<\/strong> occurs when a prepared, now outdated value later\noverwrites another change.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000427_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A reads 10 and prepares 11. B also reads 10 and prepares 11. A writes 11, then B writes 11 again. The two increments leave a total of 11 instead of 12.\" />\n<figcaption>Because both stations read 10, the second 11 overwrites the\nfirst 11.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>A <strong>critical section<\/strong> is a part of a program where\nshared data needs protection during processing. A common\n<strong>lock<\/strong> can ensure that at most one participating activity\nenters this section at a time. All participants must use the same lock\nfor the entire read-modify-write section.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Tracing two execution orders<\/h2>\n<p>In the interleaved schedule A and B both read 10. Both prepare 11. A\nwrites 11. B also writes 11. The result 11 reflects only one increment.\nIn the serial schedule, A writes 11 first. B then reads 11 and writes\n12. This model simulates both schedules and starts from 10 each\ntime.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> simulate(events):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    count <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    prepared <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> worker, action <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> events:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> action <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;read&quot;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            prepared[worker] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> count <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">elif<\/span> action <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;write&quot;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            count <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> prepared.pop(worker)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> count<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>interleaved <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;read&quot;<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;read&quot;<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;write&quot;<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;write&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>serial <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;read&quot;<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;write&quot;<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;read&quot;<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;write&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> simulate(interleaved) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">11<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> simulate(serial) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The program explicitly chooses the order in advance. This makes the\nfault reproducible without waiting for an operating system to make\nscheduling decisions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Now A adds 3 and B adds 2. Both read 10. A writes first and B writes\nsecond. What is the final value? Also give the final value for the same\nincrements in serial execution.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A team locks only each write step. Show how an update can still be\nlost. Which steps must be protected together?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A prepares 13 and B prepares 12. B overwrites 13 with 12, so the\nfinal value in the interleaved schedule is 12. In serial execution the\nresult is 15 regardless of which station increments first.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both can read 10 before the first write lock is taken. A and B then\neach write 11 in turn even though each individual write is protected.\nReading the shared value, computing the new value and writing it must\noccur under the same lock acquisition.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions, errors and limits<\/h2>\n<p>The simulation assumes that each simple read and write is\nindivisible. The composite update still consists of several steps. The\nprogramming language, library and database determine which operations\nare indivisible in practice. A lock works only if participants follow\nthe same agreement. With multiple locks, activities can block one\nanother indefinitely if each waits for a lock held by another.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>Concurrent activities can base their calculations on the same\nstarting value. Suitable synchronisation protects the update as a whole.\nA schedule table shows which order violates the data rule.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L4 – Team project: a testable\nlending system<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.4 · Explain a lost update\nand evaluate synchronisation as a model. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/12/informatik/grundlegend\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 12 – Informatik, grundlegendes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLesson reference: Inf12 LB4 · Explain a lost update and evaluate\nsynchronisation as a model. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M8-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M8-L4 – Team project: a testable lending system<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Systems and software projects<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (basic level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L2 – Planning, documenting and\nversioning software<\/a>, <a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3 – SQL\njoins, groups and missing values<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Develop a small application with\ntest data, a test plan and a division of responsibilities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A small brief for a team<\/h2>\n<p>A school club lends out cameras. It needs a prototype that assigns a\nknown, available item to a fictional member identifier. An item on loan\nremains unavailable until it is returned. On paper it is easy to add a\nsecond row. A database should reliably prevent that conflicting\nassignment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Components and a data rule<\/h2>\n<p>The input provides the item and member identifiers. The application\nlogic processes them and reports whether the request was accepted. The\ndatabase stores items and active loans. The <code>active_loan<\/code>\ntable has at most one row per item. Its <code>item_id<\/code> primary key\nmakes this rule enforceable. A foreign key ensures that each loan refers\nto a known item.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000462_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Input sends item and member identifiers to the lending logic. The logic attempts a database insert. The database allows at most one active loan per item.\" />\n<figcaption>The database enforces the rule for unique active\nloans.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>We divide responsibility among team members. One person implements\nthe schema and data rules. A second implements the application\nfunctions. A third designs test cases and documents the observed\nresults. Everyone agrees on the interface, test data and meaning of the\nreturn values before implementation. Then another person checks each\nproposed change against the agreed criteria.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Running a testable prototype<\/h2>\n<p>This example requires Python 3.11 or later with SQLite version 3.24\nor later. It begins with two known, available items. <code>book<\/code>\ntries to record a loan for a matching item. Each <code>?<\/code>\nparameter binds a value. If the item identifier is unknown, the query\nselects no row. If an active loan already exists, the conflict on\n<code>item_id<\/code> also prevents a new row. Both cases return\n<code>False<\/code>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> sqlite3<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>db <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sqlite3.<span class=\"ex\">connect<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;:memory:&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>db.execute(<span class=\"st\">&quot;PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>db.executescript(<span class=\"st\">&quot;&quot;&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">    CREATE TABLE item (<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">        item_id TEXT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">    );<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">    CREATE TABLE active_loan (<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">        item_id TEXT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES item(item_id),<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">        member_id TEXT NOT NULL<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">    );<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">    INSERT INTO item VALUES (&#39;CAM1&#39;), (&#39;CAM2&#39;);<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">&quot;&quot;&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> book(connection, item_id, member_id):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">with<\/span> connection:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        result <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> connection.execute(<span class=\"st\">&quot;&quot;&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">            INSERT INTO active_loan (item_id, member_id)<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">            SELECT item_id, ? FROM item WHERE item_id = ?<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">            ON CONFLICT(item_id) DO NOTHING<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"st\">        &quot;&quot;&quot;<\/span>, (member_id, item_id))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> result.rowcount <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> book(db, <span class=\"st\">&quot;CAM2&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;A17&quot;<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> book(db, <span class=\"st\">&quot;CAM2&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;A18&quot;<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> book(db, <span class=\"st\">&quot;CAM9&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;A18&quot;<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>loans <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> db.execute(<span class=\"st\">&quot;SELECT item_id, member_id FROM active_loan&quot;<\/span>).fetchall()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> loans <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&quot;CAM2&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;A17&quot;<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>db.close()<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Only the first assignment is stored at the end. The tests check both\nthe function’s return values and the observable data state. This would\nreveal a defect where the program reports a rejection but overwrites the\nold assignment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Design a return function that takes an item identifier. Which table\nentries should it change? Give three checks with expected results for a\nreturn, a repeated return and a subsequent new loan.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The project report says “All test cases work.” Write a specific\nverification procedure for a duplicate loan that another person can\nrepeat using the prototype.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The function runs\n<code>DELETE FROM active_loan WHERE item_id = ?<\/code> with the item\nidentifier as a parameter inside a transaction. It reports, from the\nnumber of deleted rows, whether it removed an entry. <code>CAM2<\/code>\nremains in <code>item<\/code>. After the original loan, the first return\ngives true and the second gives false. A new loan to <code>A18<\/code>\nthen gives true and leaves exactly <code>(\"CAM2\", \"A18\")<\/code> in\n<code>active_loan<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Start with available items. Call <code>book<\/code> for\n<code>CAM2<\/code> and <code>A17<\/code>, then for <code>CAM2<\/code> and\n<code>A18<\/code>. The expected results are <code>True<\/code> and\n<code>False<\/code>. The final query must return exactly\n<code>(\"CAM2\", \"A17\")<\/code>. Record the inputs, expected and observed\nresults, and the version tested.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions, errors and limits<\/h2>\n<p>The in-memory database disappears when the connection closes. The\nprototype assumes valid fictional member identifiers. It has no sign-in,\ndue dates or loan history. Before real use the team would have to\nestablish requirements for permissions, data retention, error handling\nand recovery. Tests with one local connection also do not cover a\ndistributed system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>A small team can divide work on the data model, application logic and\ntests. A central data rule protects the assignment. A repeatable test\ndemonstrates which output and table state each sample operation\nproduces.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L5 – Operating systems and\nconnected devices<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.1.4 · Develop a small\napplication with test data, a test plan and a division of\nresponsibilities. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified:\n2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/12/informatik/grundlegend\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 12 – Informatik, grundlegendes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLesson reference: Inf12 LB6 · Develop a small application with test\ndata, a test plan and a division of responsibilities. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M8-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M8-L5 – Operating systems and connected devices<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M8 – Systems and software projects<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (advanced level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L2 – Hardware, software and operating\nsystems<\/a>, <a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L3 – Concurrency and\nconsistent updates<\/a>, <a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L3 –\nProtecting data and securing accounts<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain processes, permissions\nand device lifecycles for a sensor network.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When the connection goes down<\/h2>\n<p>A greenhouse model uses a local indicator to report if it is too\nwarm. A network service can also send selected readings to a remote\ndashboard. If the internet connection goes down, the warning indicator\nshould still work. If the sensor fails, the indicator should report the\nlast known reading as stale once it is too old for a warning decision.\nThe two situations require different responses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Processes, permissions and local decisions<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>program<\/strong> describes executable instructions. A\n<strong>process<\/strong> is a running instance with execution state and\nallocated resources, such as memory and open files. The\n<strong>operating system<\/strong> allocates processing time and enforces\naccess permissions on a suitable system. Two processes can therefore\nhave different permissions for the same reading store.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000447_en.svg\"\nalt=\"The sensing service reads the sensor and updates the reading store. The local display and network service read from it through separate paths. The network service does not control the display.\" />\n<figcaption>The local data path to the display remains available if the\nnetwork service fails.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>In this model, the sensing service can read the sensor and update the\nlocal reading store. The display service can read that store and control\nthe local indicator. The separate network service has read access to\nselected readings and can transmit them. It has no write access to\nthresholds or the indicator. The <strong>principle of least\nprivilege<\/strong> grants each service the permissions it needs for its\ntask.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Checking data freshness independently of the internet<\/h2>\n<p>We use whole seconds from a local time source that moves forwards\nduring the experiment. A sample with a reading and a timestamp is fresh\nfor up to 2 seconds, given the age condition\n<code>0 &lt;= now - sampled_at &lt;= 2<\/code>. For a fresh sample, the\nstate is <code>WARN<\/code> at or above 30 °C and <code>OK<\/code> below\nthat. With no fresh sample, it is <code>STALE<\/code>. The model assumes\nvalid, finite readings.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> local_status(now, sample):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> sample <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;STALE&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    sampled_at, temperature <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sample<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    age <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> now <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> sampled_at<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> age <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;STALE&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;WARN&quot;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> temperature <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">30<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;OK&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>last_sample <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">31<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> local_status(<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, last_sample) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;WARN&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> local_status(<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>, last_sample) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;WARN&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> local_status(<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>, last_sample) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;STALE&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> local_status(<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>, (<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">29<\/span>)) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;OK&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> local_status(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;STALE&quot;<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>At second 8 the sensor reports 31 °C. The internet goes down at\nsecond 9. At second 10 the same sample is still fresh. The local\nindicator continues to warn even though the dashboard receives no\ncurrent readings. With no new sample, the local indicator reports stale\ndata at second 11. A fresh sample of 29 °C at second 12 gives\n<code>OK<\/code> again, even if the internet is still down.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The last sample was taken at second 20 and reports 30 °C. The\ninternet connection goes down. Without a fresh sample, what states does\nthe local indicator report at seconds 22 and 23?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In one design, the network service has write permissions for stored\nreadings and thresholds. Propose more suitable permissions and two\ndecisions to make when the device is retired.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At second 22 the sample is 2 seconds old and is still fresh. Exactly\n30 °C meets the warning condition, so the indicator reports\n<code>WARN<\/code>. At second 23, the sample is 3 seconds old. The state\nis <code>STALE<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Read access to the data needed for transmission is sufficient. Only\nthe sensing service needs write access to the reading store. The\nthreshold belongs in the protected local configuration. When retiring\nthe device, the team can archive or delete its stored readings in line\nwith agreed retention rules and revoke the device’s network access.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions, errors and limits<\/h2>\n<p>A local control path helps during an internet outage if the sensor\nand indicator remain reachable locally. The model alone offers no\nprotection against power loss or a sensor that reports plausible but\nincorrect fresh values. After a restart, the check begins with no valid\nsample. Device planning also includes a supported way to install updates\nand a decision on when to disconnect or replace a device that no longer\nreceives security updates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>The operating system allocates resources and supports separate\nprocess permissions. Local decisions can remain independent of the\ninternet. A separate stale-data state shows when the indicator no longer\nhas a reliable temperature status.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L1 – Analysing data and limiting\nconclusions<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/12/informatik/erhoeht\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 12 – Informatik, erhöhtes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson\nreference: Inf12 LB5 · Explain processes, permissions and device\nlifecycles for a sensor network. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/13/informatik/erhoeht\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 13 – Informatik, erhöhtes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson\nreference: Inf13 LB1 · Explain processes, permissions and device\nlifecycles for a sensor network. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
